Fixing Common Lex/YACC Build Errors When compiling a Lex + YACC program, errors often happen because the generated lexer and parser are not being connected correctly. The most common one is: undefined reference to `yylex' Let's understand why and how to fix it. 1. What does undefined reference to yylex mean? YACC/Bison generates a parser that calls: Cyylex() yylex() is supposed to be provided by Lex/Flex. So the relationship is: YACC/Bison ↓ yyparse() ↓ yylex() ↑ │ Lex/Flex If the linker cannot find yylex(), it usually means: The generated Lex file was not compiled/linked with the YACC parser. 2. Most Common Mistake You might do: Bashbison -d calc.y flex calc.l gcc calc.tab.c -o calc ❌ This is wrong. You generated: calc.tab.c lex.yy.c but compiled only: calc.tab.c Therefore yylex() from lex.yy.c is missing. Correct: Bashgcc calc.tab.c lex.yy.c -o calc 3. Complete Correct Procedure For a modern Flex + Bison program: Bashbison -d calc.y flex calc.l gcc calc.tab.c lex.yy.c -o calc ./calc That's the first thing to try. 4. Check Whether lex.yy.c Exists Run: Bashls You should see something like: calc.l calc.y calc.tab.c calc.tab.h lex.yy.c If lex.yy.c is missing, run: Bashflex calc.l again. 5. Another Common Error: yywrap You may see: undefined reference to `yywrap' This is a Flex-related issue. You have two easy solutions. Solution 1 — Add %option noyywrap At the top of your .l file: lex%option noyywrap %{ #include "calc.tab.h" %} %% [0-9]+ { yylval = atoi(yytext); return NUMBER; } [ 	] ; 
